  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14191838/assets/685c03b86db93032c8a79334c5fa46c561dd263a9aa4f52b9d7e6f41c3bb827c.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
